it's hard to figure out just what  elevates an actor's performance into  greatness sometimes it's a commitment  other times its technical skill the  sheer ability of a performer to portray  the subtleties and nuances of a  challenging character often the right  role simply fits an actor like a glove  and occasionally if everything plays out  just right all these things can happen  at once making for some of the greatest  moments in movie history these are a few  of those moments becoming less than  human there's a tremendous degree of  difficulty in performing the role of a  human stripped of their humanity someone  whose soul has been ravaged to the point  of no return and no actor has managed it  quite like Choi min-sik in Park  chan-wook's brutal masterpiece Oldboy  choice stars as oh dae-su a man out for  revenge after being imprisoned in a  hotel room under mysterious  circumstances by unknown forces there's  a little recognizable humanity and  dae-su when he first emerges from his  makeshift prison and what remains has  been stripped away by the film's climax  choice crepes down into the absolute  depths of the human soul and offers a  turn which audiences will likely never  forget no matter how much they'd like to  [Music]  digging deep Gavin O'Connor's 2011 drama  warrior is one of the unsung triumphs of  the decade what could have been a  glorified lifetime cable drama instead  turned out as a heart-wrenching story  about fathers sons and brothers joel  edgerton and nick nolte delivered their  own remarkable performances but it was  Tom Hardy who really made the movie what  it is  Hardy's character Tommy is a raw exposed  nerve of a human being ravaged by trauma  guilt and hate party masks the  character's confusion sadness and fear  with the shield of brashness that  deflects every friendly hand that comes  his way  it's a testament to Hardy's own talents  that his character never becomes  outright unlikable  despite the script giving us every  reason to hate him - some of their parts  even the most stellar ensemble casts are  out shown by one or two standout  characters pulp fiction has Vincent and  Jules the usual suspects is very much  verbals story but the best ensemble  movies are defined by a cast of  characters who all excelled together  it's rare but it happens and The  Breakfast Club is the best example of  this kind of movie it's impossible to  pinpoint a single performance among the  main cast is the best not only because  they're all just so good but because  removing one piece of the ensemble would  bring everything tumbling down now it's  eight you stay out of it excuse me sir  at seven  shut up peewee some of the finest teen  stars of the 1980s were brought together  to form the eponymous Breakfast Club  including Molly Ringwald Anthony Michael  Hall Emilio Estevez Judd Nelson and Ally  Sheedy each cast member works in perfect  tandem with the other four and every  character receives equal focus there's  no lead or standout actor and the result  is a collective performance that's  nothing less than magical nearing the  breaking point  in do the right thing Spike Lee's Mookie  is a man adrift caught in his day-to-day  life between working for a family of  racist Italian Americans and his  friendship with other in creve coeur can  Americans Mookie is a character driven  by a powerful and almost unresolvable  frustration Leigh takes the character on  a journey from selfish passivity to  insatiable fury channeling the  characters anger and slowly building  towards an inevitable breaking point  [Applause]  his performance was one of the best of  the decade and is as powerful today as  when the movie was first released  [Music]
 

